> > on both variants to research usage examples.
>
> I think of its analogy to the British vs American English.


Different dialects/languages always have different rules. That's what makes
them be different dialects. "British" and "American" English are very
similar to each other and are mutually intelligible in the vast majority of
situations, but they are still not the same.

The line between dialects and languages is not a sharp one, there is no
clear distinction. Unless we want to construct separate dialects of Lojban,
having the different spellings would add ambiguity. Ambiguity which is
unnecessary and can be avoided, at least for the time being. But isn't one
of Lojban's core principles that it is designed to avoid unnecessary
ambiguities?

.imu'omi'e .skaryzgik.


